#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230063} :activity/id #uuid "a0d14e2e-e747-5040-8791-ce5508f25e94" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048194 :topic/id #uuid "8738db80-c159-5ab6-867d-b5328c8d4b41" :topic/math-academy-id 6718} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230233 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will use Laplace transforms to handle **time-delayed forcing**, where the forcing function turns on at some time $t=a>0$.\n\nWe represent this “turning on” using the **unit step function** $u_a(t)=u(t-a)$.\n\nTo see what this looks like, consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing is $0$ for $t<2$ and becomes $e^{t-2}$ for $t\\ge 2$.\n\nTo find the Laplace transform of the forcing term, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{(t+2)-2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter this step, we proceed as usual: we take the Laplace transform of the entire differential equation, solve for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$, and then (when needed) take $\\mathcal L^{-1}$ to recover $y(t)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a57da068-df6c-54ea-98c8-714805c81391" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14744 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33e4bd2f-e595-58ed-9a93-727ced833dc4" :step/math-academy-id 600519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230231 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' - 4y = u_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nFirst, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the linearity properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{'}-4y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{'}}-4L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathcal L\\{y'\\},$ we use the derivative formula and the initial condition $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s).\n$$\n\nTo compute the right-hand side, we note that\n\n$$\nu_2(t)\\cdot e^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=e^{t-2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the **second shifting theorem** (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}=e^{-as}\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{(t+2)-2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation and solve for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$ will give us the solution to our initial value problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "3066f75f-f843-5283-a3be-923fa13ce2dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14715 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c79dc0af-fb98-5c4a-95f9-e6bf263d96dc" :step/math-academy-id 600520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228946 :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317322}] :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "e11300e8-c33d-57ec-95a3-cbca3b7d9c73" :step/math-academy-id 600521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228947 :question/id #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-21861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050768}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317330}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"} :step/id #uuid "f45c78a8-04bf-5a0d-9b2a-da4fc8eb54e6" :step/math-academy-id 600525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230234 :tutorial/content "When working with time-delayed forcing, a delay often appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nSuppose $F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}.$ Then the second shifting theorem can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\} = e^{-as}\\,F(s), \\qquad a>0.\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can read this rule *backwards* as an inverse Laplace transform statement:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a), \\qquad a>0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $Y(s)$ contains a term like $e^{-as}F(s)$, we can handle it using the following steps.\n\n- **Step 1:** Compute $f(t)=\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Shift the function by replacing $t$ with $(t-a)$.\n\n- **Step 3:** Multiply by the unit step function $u(t-a)$.\n\nWe consider the concrete example below." :tutorial/id #uuid "f9ee51be-d7c5-57bd-9302-41618fa57ab0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14745 :tutorial/title "Taking the Inverse Transform and Applying the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "03a9dbab-bfb2-5a17-8b2a-da80a0acc614" :step/math-academy-id 600529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230240 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 3y = u_\\pi(t)\\cdot \\cos(t-\\pi)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_\\pi(t)=u(t-\\pi)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAssume we are given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{2}{s+3} + \\dfrac{s\\,e^{-\\pi s}}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find the function $g(t).$ To do this, we'll need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ We can use the following standard results:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \n\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad\ns > 0.\n$$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-\\pi s}$ and identify its rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll find the inverse transform in three steps.\n\n**Step 1. Partial fraction decomposition**\n\nTo handle the rational function $F(s),$ we use *partial fraction decomposition*. Since the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $(s^2+1),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{s}{(s^2+1)(s+3)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+3} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns = A(s^2+1) + (Bs+C)(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3 = A\\bigl((-3)^2+1\\bigr) + 0 \\implies 10A = -3 \\implies A = -\\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + B \\implies B = -A = \\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = 3B + C \\implies 1 = 3\\!\\left(\\dfrac{3}{10}\\right) + C \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)= -\\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s+3} + \\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{s}{s^2+1} + \\dfrac{1}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{s^2+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2. Finding the inverse of the base function**\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}t+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(-3{e}^{-3t}+3\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3. Applying the time-shifting property**\n\nFinally, we use the *second shifting theorem* to handle the $e^{-\\pi s}$ factor. The theorem states:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\pi.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-\\pi )f(t-\\pi ) & =u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3\\operatorname{cos}(t-\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}(t-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi )\\cdot [-3{e}^{-3(t-\\pi )}+3(-\\operatorname{cos}t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}t)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos(t-\\pi)=-\\cos t$ and $\\sin(t-\\pi)=-\\sin t,$ we simplify:\n\n$$\nu(t-\\pi)f(t-\\pi) = \\dfrac{1}{10}u(t-\\pi)\\cdot \\left(-3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t\\right).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion: Identifying g(t)**\n\nThis result is the inverse transform of the second term in $Y(s).$ The problem statement gives the complete solution in the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = 2e^{-3t} + \\dfrac{1}{10} u(t-\\pi)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nThe first term, $2e^{-3t},$ is the inverse transform of $\\dfrac{2}{s+3}.$ By comparing our calculated term with the form above, we can identify **$g(t)$**:\n\n$$\ng(t)= -3e^{-3(t-\\pi)} - 3\\cos t - \\sin t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea2c953f-32af-54a8-abd8-8f2f9a59c107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14882 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "c822adae-6fde-5b63-ae68-0fa00820fcc7" :step/math-academy-id 607925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230066}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229269}]}
